{
  "book": "LUK",
  "chapter": 7,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-042f675c344938e1": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-762",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "It was no unusual thing for one man to build a synagogue at his own expense. If, as in this case, a Gentile built the sacred edifice, the Jews had no scruples in receiving the gift, even if he did not become a proselyte, as some suppose this centurion to have been. They held that…"
      },
      "c-8fd906be60801339": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-763",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Now when he came nigh to the gate of the city, behold, there was a dead man carried out, the only son of his mother, and she was a widow: and much people of the city was with her."
      },
      "c-bc1dd63f966126e5": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-764",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The bier is a wooden frame, partly resembling a coffin and partly a hand barrow. The deceased is arrayed in grave-clothes, the ankles are bound, the hands are laid on the breast, and a shawl is thrown across the face. Miss Rogers says of a bier she saw: “It was a painted wooden s…"
      },
      "c-df7b4c500b5f6fad": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-765",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This was no unusual practice among the Jews, and was also customary among the Greeks and Romans. It was a mark of affection and of reverence. It was also the practice of supplicants, and of those who had an important request to present. Kissing the feet of princes was a token of…"
      }
    },
